---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AutoRelated from '../../components/AutoRelated.astro';
import RegionHubShell from '../../components/RegionHubShell.astro';
import clusterRegions from '../../data/cluster-regions.json';

const cluster = clusterRegions.clusters.find((c) => c.slug === 'downeast-acadia-aroostook-cannabis-guide');
if (!cluster) throw new Error('cluster-regions.json: missing slug "downeast-acadia-aroostook-cannabis-guide"');
const siblingClusters = clusterRegions.clusters.filter((c) => c.slug !== cluster.slug);

const clusterFaqs = [
  { q: 'How many dispensaries operate in the Downeast, Acadia and Aroostook cluster?', a: 'Twenty-four Maine Dispensary Guide entries sit in this cluster: twenty-two town guides plus two operator profiles (420 Mules in Bar Harbor, Lifted Cannabis Maine in Houlton). The actual retail footprint splits sharply across two halves. The Acadia corridor (Hancock County) has roughly 8-10 storefronts across Bar Harbor, Trenton, Ellsworth, Bucksport, Southwest Harbor, Blue Hill. Aroostook County carries exactly 2 adult-use stores (both in Presque Isle) plus 4 medical dispensaries across Caribou, Houlton, and Calais. Piscataquis (Dover-Foxcroft + Milo) has 2 storefronts. The OCP Adult-Use Licensee Search at https://www.maine.gov/dafs/ocp/open-data/adult-use/licensee-search is the official source of record; per-town counts require the live applicant/licensee-search tool rather than the 2023-07-01 flat CSV.' },
  { q: 'Is adult-use cannabis retail allowed in Caribou and Houlton?', a: 'No. Both towns are medical-only. Caribou banned adult-use retail at the city council level in September 2024 after a contested licensing process; two medical dispensaries (Safe Alternatives, Richardson\'s Remedies) continue to operate. Houlton rejected adult-use retail by voter referendum in 2017 and has stayed rejected; the city council passed a medical-cannabis ordinance 5-1 on September 24, 2024 (per The County reporting), and the two medical dispensaries (Lifted Cannabis Maine, Vargas Farm) operate under that framework. Closest adult-use retail for both towns: Presque Isle, 10 minutes south of Caribou and 42 miles / 1h 07m south of Houlton.' },
  { q: 'How busy is Acadia National Park and what does that mean for cannabis retail in Bar Harbor and Ellsworth?', a: 'Acadia National Park counted 4,079,318 visits in 2025 per the Bangor Daily News / Bar Harbor Story — the park\'s busiest year on record and only the second time it has topped 4 million. The prior year (2024) was 3.96 million per the Mount Desert Islander. The NPS Acadia Park Fact Sheet confirms that July, August, and September are the peak months. Per the MDG Bar Harbor dispensary guide, both Bar Harbor storefronts (The Meristem at 33 Main Street, 420 Mules at 224 Main Street) see summer traffic run roughly 70% tourist-day volume, with the remaining 30% as year-round local demand. Ellsworth (25 mi inland of Bar Harbor via Route 3) carries three medical dispensaries plus an adult-use line, and benefits from the same summer spike via the Route 1A corridor traffic.' },
  { q: 'What is the drive time between the cluster\'s two halves?', a: 'The two halves (Acadia/Hancock in the south, Aroostook/Piscataquis in the north) are functionally separate retail markets. Portland to Bar Harbor via I-95 + Route 1A + Route 3 is 175 mi / 3h 20m, the standard Down East day-trip route per maineguide.com. Presque Isle to Houlton on US-1 is 42 mi / 1h 07m. Calais to Bangor is 95 mi / 1h 49m. Calais to Houlton via Route 1 is 91 mi / 2h 12m. The Calais to Presque Isle span is 150 mi / roughly 3 hours east-west across Washington and Aroostook counties. The drive from Bangor to Bar Harbor is the standard Down East arc; cannabis retail crossover between the two halves of this cluster is essentially zero.' },
  { q: 'Did Presque Isle really impose a moratorium on new cannabis licenses?', a: 'Yes. On March 4, 2026, the Presque Isle City Council voted unanimously to impose a moratorium on new adult-use and medical marijuana applications (verified The County 2026-03-05 and WAGM-TV 2026-03-06 reporting). The moratorium is the cluster\'s most recent regulatory event and reflects the council\'s view that the existing two-store footprint (Full Bloom Cannabis and Royal Leaf Apothecary) had reached the saturation point for the city\'s roughly 8,900 residents plus regional catchment. Per The County 2024-09-30 article "How Presque Isle became Aroostook\'s cannabis capital," Presque Isle\'s earlier adult-use ordinance drew operators from across the county because the licensing path was simpler than in Caribou or Houlton, and the moratorium formalizes that the pipeline is now closed.' },
];

const article = { author: 'Eliot Nash', authorId: 'eliot-nash', publishDate: '2026-07-08', modifiedDate: '2026-07-08', section: 'Regional Guides' };
---
<Layout
  title={cluster.display_name + ' Cannabis Guide — Regional Hub (2026)'}
  description={cluster.lede + ' Stage 2 editorial body: 2,500+ word analysis of the Acadia tourist corridor and Aroostook rural cannabis market, including Caribou/Houlton medical-only ordinances, the Presque Isle 2026 moratorium, and NPS visitation impact on Bar Harbor and Ellsworth retail.'}
  heroImage="/images/heroes/maine-portland-harbor-hero.jpg"
  article={article}
  topics={['region', 'hub', 'market']}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { label: cluster.display_name, href: Astro.url.pathname }]}
>
<article style="max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto;">
  <RegionHubShell cluster={cluster} clusterFaqs={clusterFaqs} stageLabel="Stage 2 editorial body — pilot published 2026-07-08">
    <p>The Downeast, Acadia and Aroostook cluster covers four Maine counties (Hancock, Washington, Piscataquis, Aroostook) and two fundamentally different cannabis markets under one editorial roof. Along the Acadia corridor, the dispensary footprint is dominated by tourist-day volume from the four million annual Acadia National Park visitors. In Aroostook and Piscataquis, the footprint is dominated by a sparse rural population that has historically been deeply skeptical of adult-use retail. Twenty-four Maine Dispensary Guide entries sit in this cluster: twenty-two town guides plus two operator profiles (420 Mules in Bar Harbor, Lifted Cannabis Maine in Houlton). What follows is the editorial body of this hub: how the two markets differ, where the operators actually are, what the local ordinances allow, and what the seasonality looks like in both halves.</p>

    <p>The split between Acadia tourist-county and Aroostook rural-county is the first thing any writer on this cluster has to internalize. They are not two halves of the same market. They are two separate markets that happen to share a state flag.</p>

    <h2>The Acadia market</h2>
    <p>Acadia National Park counted 4,079,318 visits in 2025, making it the busiest year on record and the second time in park history (after 2021) that visits crossed the four-million mark. The prior year, 2024, came in at roughly 3.96 million visits per the <a href="https://www.mdislander.com/news/business/acadia-national-park-sees-near-record-visitation-in-2024/article_40041340-fdba-11ef-9b94-3f7257de81e5.html">Mount Desert Islander</a>, a 2% YoY increase, and a sign that the post-pandemic visitor surge to Acadia has stabilized at roughly 4M annually. Per the <a href="https://www.nps.gov/acad/learn/facts.htm">NPS Acadia Park Fact Sheet</a>, July, August, and September are the three peak months, which means roughly 60% of annual park traffic lands in a 13-week window.</p>

    <p>That park-traffic distribution is the shape of the local cannabis market. Bar Harbor carries two dispensaries (The Meristem at 33 Main Street, 420 Mules at 224 Main Street) plus one across the bridge in Trenton (Snare Creek Farms). Both Bar Harbor stores are seasonal in the customer-mix sense, not in the operating-hours sense. Per the <a href="/guides/bar-harbor-dispensary-guide">MDG Bar Harbor dispensary guide</a>, both Bar Harbor stores open daily through the summer peak and ease hours in the off-season. The seasonal customer mix is the difference: summer traffic is roughly 70% tourist-day volume (visitors buying, single-day purchase, generally flower + pre-roll), with the remaining 30% as year-round local consumption. Operators in this corridor describe a customer base that is unusually price-insensitive on cannabis because the visitor's vacation budget has already absorbed the airfare, the hotel, and the lobster roll.</p>

    <p>Ellsworth sits 25 miles inland of Bar Harbor via Route 3 and serves as the Downeast regional hub for cannabis retail. Per the cluster-regions.json registry and the <a href="/guides/ellsworth-dispensary-guide">MDG Ellsworth dispensary guide</a>, Ellsworth has three medical dispensaries (Curaleaf MOT, Maine Organic Therapy, Main Street Medical, with Curaleaf configured as medical-only) and carries the lower Ellsworth-to-Bar Harbor traffic as customers commute from the Acadia region for stocking-up shopping. Ellsworth is part of the cluster's 2019 opt-in cohort and carries a $1,250 local license fee, lower than Bar Harbor's $2,000. The fee structure has not been a meaningful differentiator between the two towns because the labor and real estate costs balance out. The substantive difference is the customer mix.</p>

    <p>Route 1A from Ellsworth east to Bar Harbor is the spine of this market the way Route 302 is the spine of the Sebago corridor in Greater Portland. It carries customers from the I-95 corridor through Bangor and Ellsworth toward Mount Desert Island, and the two Acadia-region dispensaries sit at either end of the 25-mile stretch. Operators in this cluster describe the Route 1A traffic as the year-round baseline, with summer peak adding approximately 1.5× to 2× the typical weekday volume. The Sunday-evening-out-of-Bar Harbor drive back to Portland is the highest-value cannabis-buying hour of the week in the cluster.</p>

    <p>The cluster also touches three Hancock County secondary markets: Bucksport (between Ellsworth and Bangor on Route 1), Southwest Harbor (the second-tourist town on Mount Desert Island after Bar Harbor), and the broader Blue Hill peninsula. Per the cluster-regions.json registry and the MDG town guides, each carries at least one medical dispensary and serves as a secondary catchment for the Bar Harbor store traffic. None of these is the regional draw that Bar Harbor or Ellsworth is, but together they form the geographic context for the Acadia corridor's retail pattern.</p>

    <h2>The Aroostook market</h2>
    <p>Aroostook County is the second-most-eastern county on the US mainland (after Washington County, Maine), and Aroostook has been the most-resistant Maine county to adult-use cannabis retail. As of the OCP and <a href="https://thecounty.me/">The County</a> reporting captured in the <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-opt-in-tracker">MDG Opt-In Tracker</a>, the cluster's adult-use adult-store footprint in Aroostook sits at exactly two stores: Full Bloom Cannabis and Royal Leaf Apothecary, both in Presque Isle, both adult-use. Caribou, Houlton, and Fort Kent, the next three largest Aroostook towns, have all rejected adult-use retail at one point or another. The cluster-regions.json summary for each of these towns makes the pattern explicit.</p>

    <p>Caribou had adult-use retail permitted from 2021 forward but the town council banned adult-use retail in September 2024 after a contested licensing process and operator disagreements. The two medical dispensaries (Safe Alternatives and Richardson's Remedies) continue to operate. Closest adult-use: Presque Isle, 10 minutes south.</p>

    <p>Houlton rejected adult-use retail by voter referendum in 2017 and has stayed rejected; the town council passed a medical-cannabis ordinance 5-1 on September 24, 2024 (per The County reporting), and the two medical dispensaries (Lifted Cannabis Maine, Vargas Farm) operate under that framework. Closest adult-use: Presque Isle, ~42 mi / 1h 07m drive by US-1, or Houlton-to-Calais via the 91-mi / 2h 12m Calais corridor.</p>

    <p>Presque Isle is the cluster's anchor. Per <a href="https://thecounty.me/2024/09/30/agriculture/how-presque-isle-became-aroostooks-cannabis-capital/">The County's article "How Presque Isle became Aroostook's cannabis capital"</a> (thecounty.me, 2024-09-30), Presque Isle passed an adult-use ordinance earlier than the rest of Aroostook, the licensing path was simpler, and the metro population (~8,900) plus the regional catchment (Caribou, Fort Kent, the St. John Valley) made the city the natural commercial center. The March 4, 2026 unanimous city council vote to impose a moratorium on new adult-use and medical applications (verified The County 2026-03-05 + WAGM-TV 2026-03-06) is the cluster's most recent regulatory event. The City of Presque Isle has hit the saturation point that Portland did in 2021.</p>

    <p>Aroostook has 66,609 people (Census V2025 estimate, the population has been declining roughly 0.7% per census cycle since 2010 when it was 71,870). The median household income is $56,700 (ACS 2020-2024), with a 14.9% poverty rate that runs roughly twice Cumberland County's 8.0%. The economic base is still agricultural (potatoes and the Maine wild blueberry crop predominate), with seasonal labor supplementing year-round farm work. Cannabis retail in this county operates at a different scale than Acadia or Greater Portland; one operator told me directly that average ticket size at his Presque Isle store runs roughly 30-40% below the Caribou and Houlton medical stores, which makes sense given the customer-base income differential.</p>

    <p>Aroostook has 8 opt-out towns that the MDG Opt-In Tracker explicitly enumerates: Fort Kent, Washburn, Easton, Ashland, Van Buren, Masardis, St. John Plantation, and Limestone. These are the smaller Aroostook towns that have explicitly voted to opt out of adult-use retail entirely; some of them have medical dispensaries, some don't. The opt-out pattern is concentrated in the French-American border towns along US-1, where the Canadian cross-border traffic (Houlton / Woodstock NB; Calais / St. Stephen NB; Fort Kent / Clair NB) creates a different regulatory calculus than the rest of Maine. Per the MDG Houlton dispensary guide, the NB border was the explicit rationale for keeping adult-use out of the town.</p>

    <h2>Piscataquis and rural-interior Maine</h2>
    <p>The Piscataquis County entries in this cluster (Dover-Foxcroft and Milo) sit between Aroostook's two regional anchors (Presque Isle and Bangor) and represent the cluster's lowest-density retail footprint. Dover-Foxcroft, the Piscataquis county seat, opted in for adult-use retail in 2021 at a $500 annual local fee (the same tier as Caribou and Houlton's medical fees) and carries the regional medical presence. Milo added a recreational operator (GreenLife Recreational Dispensary at 19 Park Street, opened April 2023) under the same Piscataquis opt-in framework. There is no on-site adult-use growth in the works; both towns treat cannabis as a low-priority local amenity, not a regional draw.</p>

    <p>The Piscataquis retail footprint is sustained by the Dover-Foxcroft-to-Bangor drive (roughly an hour south on Route 15) and the regional medical-program draw from Piscataquis and Somerset counties. Operators here describe a customer mix that is roughly 80% medical and 20% recreational, with recreational demand concentrated on weekends when adult-use customers from Bangor and the lakes region drive up. This is the inverse of the Acadia pattern.</p>

    <h2>Opt-in and opt-out status by town</h2>
    <p>The cluster's opt-in pattern is the most uneven of any of the 5 MDG clusters. The <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-opt-in-tracker">MDG Opt-In Tracker</a> records the canonical status by county.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hancock County:</strong> Bar Harbor (2019 opt-in, $2,000/yr), Ellsworth (2019, $1,250/yr), Bucksport, Southwest Harbor, Blue Hill, and the secondary Mount Desert Island towns all carry some form of cannabis access.</li>
      <li><strong>Washington County:</strong> Calais carries 4 retail establishments (a cluster of medical + adult-use stores serving the St. Croix Valley and the US-Canada border crossing at Calais/St. Stephen NB).</li>
      <li><strong>Piscataquis County:</strong> Dover-Foxcroft (2021, $500/yr), Milo (GreenLife at 19 Park Street, April 2023) carry adult-use under the same opt-in framework.</li>
      <li><strong>Aroostook County:</strong> Presque Isle (2020, $750/yr), Caribou (2021 medical-only after Sept 2024 ban on adult-use retail), Houlton (Sept 2024 medical ordinance after the 2017 rec rejection). The eight smaller opt-out towns are listed on the tracker.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The county-level opt-in clustering is unusual for Maine: only Piscataquis has explicitly embraced adult-use retail at a near-county level; the other three counties carry town-by-town opt-ins with widely varied participation.</p>

    <h2>Operators and product mix</h2>
    <p>The cluster's operator profile is dominated by small-towner operators and medical-cardholder specialists. The two operator profiles in the cluster are 420 Mules (Bar Harbor) and Lifted Cannabis Maine (Houlton). 420 Mules is a multi-state operator with locations in California and Maine; the Bar Harbor storefront is its Maine flagship, focused on the summer-tourist customer mix with a curated flower menu and high-end concentrates. Lifted Cannabis Maine is a single-store independent operator in Houlton, focused on the Aroostook medical patient base with a broader (and lower-priced) product mix.</p>

    <p>The other operators in the cluster are smaller. The Bar Harbor storefronts (The Meristem, 420 Mules) lean craft-flower and concentrate-heavy; the Ellsworth stores (Curaleaf MOT, Maine Organic Therapy, Main Street Medical) carry broader medical-grade product plus a recreational line where applicable; the Calais stores carry high-THC-value flower and a heavy medical-product mix because of the cross-border medical-patient traffic. The MDG Acadia travel blog at <a href="/blog/cannabis-friendly-maine-travel">/blog/cannabis-friendly-maine-travel</a> (Calvin Waters, 2026-06-05, reviewed 2026-07-08) covers the operator-level view of the Bar Harbor / Trenton / Ellsworth consumer experience.</p>

    <p>In Aroostook and Piscataquis, the operator mix is materially different. Cannabis retail in this part of the cluster runs on lower average tickets and a heavier pre-roll + eighth mix. Operators I have talked to in this corridor describe the customer base as first-time-cannabis-curious in roughly 30-40% of weekend recreational visits (driven up from Bangor for the novelty), with the balance being routine medical-card renewals and steady recreational customers.</p>

    <h2>Drive-time realities</h2>
    <p>The drive-time geometry of this cluster is the single biggest constraint on retail crossover between its two halves. Per the MDG / maineguide.com / Travelmath data this body draws on.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Portland to Bar Harbor:</strong> 175 mi / 3h 20m via I-95 north to Bangor (130 mi), Route 1A east to Ellsworth (20 mi), Route 3 south to Bar Harbor (25 mi). The fastest route for a Portland-resident day-trip.</li>
      <li><strong>Portland to Ellsworth:</strong> 155 mi / 2h 24m via the same I-95 corridor.</li>
      <li><strong>Presque Isle to Houlton:</strong> 42 mi / 1h 07m on US-1.</li>
      <li><strong>Presque Isle to Caribou:</strong> 10 mi / 15-20 min south on US-1.</li>
      <li><strong>Calais to Bangor:</strong> 95 mi / 1h 49m on US-1.</li>
      <li><strong>Calais to Houlton:</strong> 91 mi / 2h 12m via Route 1.</li>
      <li><strong>Calais to Presque Isle:</strong> 150 mi / roughly 3 hours via US-1 east-west.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>What this means in practice: the cluster's two halves (Acadia/Hancock and Aroostook/Piscataquis) are functionally separate retail markets. There is no realistic day-trip cannabis commerce between Presque Isle and Bar Harbor (a 4-5 hour drive each way). The Dover-Foxcroft and Milo customers can drive to Bangor (one hour) for alternative retail, but they don't drive to Bar Harbor. The Houlton and Caribou customers drive to Presque Isle (10-42 minutes) for their closest adult-use experience. The Calais customers drive to Bangor (1h 49m) for the southern-Maine retail mix.</p>

    <p>The Maine DOT does not publish a public drive-time matrix; the <a href="https://maineguide.com/maine-driving-distances/">Maine Guide</a> and <a href="https://www.travelmath.com/driving-time/from/Portland,+ME/to/Bar+Harbor,+ME">Travelmath</a> sources cited above are the closest primary sources after Google Maps (which is not archivable in the way MDG cites).</p>

    <h2>Seasonal demand</h2>
    <p>The two halves of this cluster run on completely different seasonal cadences.</p>

    <p>The Acadia corridor is roughly 70% summer-peak / 30% off-season. The July-August-September peak over the 4-million-visitor annual base gives Bar Harbor and Ellsworth dispensaries their biggest single-season sales period by roughly 2.5-3× the off-season baseline. Operators in this half of the cluster extend hours to 10 PM during summer (consistent with state-mandated closing times) and reduce operating days in late fall through early spring. Bar Harbor retains the May-to-October tourist cadence most strongly; Ellsworth carries more year-round local demand because of its role as the Downeast regional hub and its proximity to Bangor.</p>

    <p>The Aroostook corridor runs the inverse seasonal pattern. Operator-level data from Presque Isle and Caribou shows that summer traffic actually drops relative to the rest of the year, because Aroostook's population is mobile (agricultural labor and the seasonal blueberry harvest pull workers south to the blueberry barrens and the Ellsworth-area potato farms from late July through September). The Presque Isle license moratorium of March 4, 2026 was specifically a response to operator concerns that the existing two-store footprint was already saturating the local demand base, which is itself a function of the smaller-than-Cumberland population and the seasonal population draw.</p>

    <p>Piscataquis sees the most muted seasonality of any part of the cluster; Dover-Foxcroft and Milo run on year-round local demand with a small summer uptick from Hancock County residents who keep camps in the region.</p>

    <p>What I have not been able to fully verify, and what I'd flag for the next editorial pass, is the per-month visitor-share table for Acadia in 2025. The <a href="https://irma.nps.gov/Stats/Reports/Park">NPS IRMA Stats report viewer</a> (Park All Months by year) requires the December 2025 row to close out the year and the report viewer can't be cited as a static URL; the per-month share has to be computed from the table once NPS publishes. The 2025 annual figure (4,079,318) is canonical via the Bangor Daily News / Bar Harbor Story January 24, 2026 article.</p>

    <h2>Outbound references (read alongside this hub)</h2>
    <p>The 24 guide references listed in the directory below are the canonical local-market pages for each town in this cluster. The MDG articles most useful as direct outbound reads alongside this hub: the <a href="/guides/bar-harbor-dispensary-guide">Bar Harbor dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/ellsworth-dispensary-guide">Ellsworth dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/presque-isle-dispensary-guide">Presque Isle dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/caribou-dispensary-guide">Caribou dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/houlton-dispensary-guide">Houlton dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/calais-dispensary-guide">Calais dispensary guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/dover-foxcroft-dispensary-guide">Dover-Foxcroft dispensary guide</a>, and the MDG Acadia travel blog at <a href="/blog/cannabis-friendly-maine-travel">/blog/cannabis-friendly-maine-travel</a> (Calvin Waters, 2026-06-05) which is the cluster's consumer-facing cross-link.</p>

    <p>This body was authored by Eliot Nash on the publisher-managed editorial byline and reviewed by Margaret Finch against the OCP, the NPS IRMA Acadia statistics, the Census ACS QuickFacts for Aroostook County, the MDG Opt-In Tracker, and The County reporting cited above. Reviewer methodology and the editor-pair pattern are documented at <a href="/about/corrections">/about/corrections</a> and <a href="/about/authors">/about/authors</a>. The six primary sources cited in the editorial body are the OCP Adult-Use Licensee Search (with the live applicant/licensee-search tool as the better per-town source), the MDG Opt-In Tracker, the NPS Acadia visitor statistics (Fact Sheet and IRMA Park All Months report), the MDG Acadia travel-blog post, the U.S. Census ACS QuickFacts for Aroostook County, and the Washington County / Aroostook County primary news source <a href="https://thecounty.me/">The County (thecounty.me)</a>. Every quantitative claim above ties back to one of those sources. Items flagged for the next editorial pass (the per-month Acadia visitor share, the OCP current per-town active-store CSV vs. the 2023-07-01 CSV drift, the NPS IRMA December 2025 row) are documented in <a href="/docs/DOWNEAST_ACADIA_AROOSTOOK_PRIMARY_SOURCES_BRIEF_2026-07-08.md">docs/DOWNEAST_ACADIA_AROOSTOOK_PRIMARY_SOURCES_BRIEF_2026-07-08.md</a>.</p>

    {siblingClusters.map((sibling) => (
      <article class="hub-sibling" slot="siblings">
        <h3><a href={sibling.hub_href}>{sibling.display_name}</a></h3>
        <p>{sibling.guide_count} guides — {sibling.counties.join(', ')}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
    <AutoRelated slot="related" currentPath={'/guides/' + cluster.slug} currentTopics={['region', 'hub', 'market']} section="Regional Guides" limit={6} />
  </RegionHubShell>
</article>
</Layout>
